WEBVTT

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:30.155
The losing teams of the main match,
Team KAIST and Team SNU in STEM,

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:33.575
will now begin today's Deathmatch.

00:00:33.659 --> 00:00:36.036
It's time we got a bit of action.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:39.039
Let's focus on keeping our cool, guys.

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:42.584
Team KAIST got dragged
into the Deathmatch because of us,

00:00:42.668 --> 00:00:44.878
despite coming in second,

00:00:44.962 --> 00:00:48.298
so I felt like we owed them
for falling behind.

00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:52.803
But in a Deathmatch,
there's no room for such sentimentality.

00:00:53.262 --> 00:00:55.013
All right.

00:00:57.224 --> 00:00:59.393
I'd be lying
if I said I don't blame them.

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:04.189
But I'd rather take this as a chance
to prove that KAIST is the best in STEM.

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:06.525
Today's Deathmatch is...

00:01:09.027 --> 00:01:11.613
Lucky 21.

00:01:11.697 --> 00:01:12.698
Lucky 21?

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:14.658
- Will it involve numbers?
- Probably.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.785
- Sounds like blackjack, no?
- Yeah.

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:18.787
The goal in that game
is to get 21 points as well.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:23.834
In Lucky 21,
you will be presented with a 7-by-7 board.

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:27.379
You will be given time
to memorize the 49 numbered tiles

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:30.382
and then take turns to obtain them.

00:01:30.465 --> 00:01:36.221
Your task is to make a set of 3 tiles
that add up to 21 to earn points.

00:01:37.723 --> 00:01:38.765
It's a memorization game.

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:41.810
Jong-yun has an edge in memorization.

00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:44.980
ACED THE MEMORIZATION QUIZ IN THE PRE-TEST
I felt certain I would win.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:50.027
The numbered tiles include
special tiles and basic tiles.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.154
The special tiles include
the number 7 tiles

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:54.281
and the ones marked with a clover.

00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:59.578
There are 4 tiles of the number 7
and 7 tiles marked with a clover.

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:03.290
The remaining 38 tiles are the basic ones.

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.710
They named it "lucky"
because of the clover.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:08.587
During the match,

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.673
only the backside of the tiles
will be displayed.

00:02:11.757 --> 00:02:15.802
At the outset, one game piece
will be placed in the center of the board.

00:02:15.886 --> 00:02:18.347
You can move the piece

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:21.433
WITHIN THE 30-SECOND LIMIT
up to 3 cells at your turn.

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:25.937
But the first player can move it
only one cell on their initial turn.

00:02:26.021 --> 00:02:30.025
Additionally, you cannot reverse
your opponent's previous move,

00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:32.861
or move the piece diagonally.

00:02:33.487 --> 00:02:35.280
Once the piece reaches your destination,

00:02:35.364 --> 00:02:38.950
you must perform
one of the following four actions.

00:02:39.034 --> 00:02:40.786
Your first option is "checking."

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.579
If you ask for checking,

00:02:42.663 --> 00:02:45.707
the tile's number will be shown
to you and your opponent.

00:02:45.791 --> 00:02:49.753
Then it will be returned to its place
with the backside showing.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.713
They should avoid that option, right?

00:02:51.797 --> 00:02:53.965
You see it together,
so that should be your final resort.

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:57.010
If you're good enough,
you won't ask for that option.

00:02:57.094 --> 00:02:59.012
Yes, if you're good enough.

00:02:59.096 --> 00:03:00.847
Your second option is "obtaining."

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:04.017
You can obtain the tile
once the piece reaches its stop.

00:03:04.101 --> 00:03:08.271
You can only keep
up to four tiles at a time.

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:11.483
What happens if you get more?

00:03:11.942 --> 00:03:14.486
Your third option is "discarding."

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:16.571
Oh, so that's an option.

00:03:16.905 --> 00:03:19.032
You can take the piece
to a cell without a tile

00:03:19.116 --> 00:03:23.036
and discard one of the tiles
in your possession.

00:03:23.954 --> 00:03:26.081
- That changes the layout.
- Right.

00:03:26.832 --> 00:03:28.041
That's wild.

00:03:28.125 --> 00:03:31.211
If the other guy takes a tile from here
and discards it over there,

00:03:31.294 --> 00:03:34.214
- that changes the whole setup.
- This reminds me of Match and Mix.

00:03:34.297 --> 00:03:35.549
GAME FROM SEASON 1

00:03:35.632 --> 00:03:37.843
The board keeps changing
from the one in your memory.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.387
Your fourth option is "taking a pass."

00:03:40.762 --> 00:03:45.600
You can choose not to do anything
and complete your turn.

00:03:45.684 --> 00:03:47.602
Proceed with the game

00:03:47.686 --> 00:03:50.981
until the sum of 3 tiles
in your possession reaches 21.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:54.359
Then, you can log the set.

00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:57.863
You win one point
if your set consists of the basic tiles.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:00.991
If your set is a trio of Tile 7s

00:04:01.074 --> 00:04:03.493
or a trio of clover tiles,

00:04:03.577 --> 00:04:05.996
you win two points.

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:09.916
If your set contains
both basic and special tiles,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.252
you win one point.

00:04:12.836 --> 00:04:15.046
Keep winning points
by logging suitable sets.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:20.218
The first to secure four points
wins the round.

00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:22.262
Your priority should be Tile 7s.

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:25.807
You can double the point
with a set of special tiles.

00:04:25.891 --> 00:04:28.977
It might not be a bad idea
to memorize just the special tiles.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.562
That's one possible strategy.

00:04:30.645 --> 00:04:33.523
Suppose you take the tiles
and log your sets non-stop.

00:04:33.607 --> 00:04:35.150
Two-pointers will get you a quicker win.

00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:39.738
If a player obtains two Tile 7s,
the other has to claim the remaining ones.

00:04:39.821 --> 00:04:41.448
Otherwise, they lose two points.

00:04:41.531 --> 00:04:44.159
You can make
one special request per round,

00:04:44.242 --> 00:04:47.329
either the "Move 5 Cells" card
or the "Discard 1 Tile" card.

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:50.457
The choice is up to you.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:55.378
If you use the Move 5 Cells card,
you can move the piece up to 5 cells.

00:04:56.046 --> 00:04:57.839
If you use the Discard 1 Tile card,

00:04:57.923 --> 00:05:00.634
you can place the tile
on any empty cell of your choice

00:05:00.717 --> 00:05:02.761
without consuming your turn.

00:05:02.844 --> 00:05:03.804
That may be more helpful.

00:05:03.887 --> 00:05:06.181
You can discard a tile
when the sum isn't 21.

00:05:06.556 --> 00:05:08.517
There will be three rounds in total.

00:05:08.892 --> 00:05:12.479
Rounds 1 and 2 require different players,
but anyone can participate in Round 3.

00:05:12.562 --> 00:05:14.397
- Let's go get them.
- All right.

00:05:14.481 --> 00:05:15.941
Your time has come.

00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:17.859
Good luck.

00:05:17.943 --> 00:05:22.572
As Team KAIST apprehended the culprit
in Signal Investigation 3 in less time,

00:05:22.656 --> 00:05:27.077
they will decide which team goes first.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.871
Both teams,
please start discussing your strategies.

00:05:29.955 --> 00:05:32.541
You'll be given 20 minutes.

00:05:33.208 --> 00:05:36.002
Hit me up with ideas.

00:05:36.086 --> 00:05:37.587
Should we go first or second?

00:05:37.671 --> 00:05:41.132
If we go first,
we'll literally get one more tile.

00:05:41.216 --> 00:05:43.677
Can we make them go first
and take a Tile 7 on our turn?

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.428
- You want us to go second?
- Yes.

00:05:45.512 --> 00:05:49.599
If you think about it,
the best tile to secure first is a Tile 7.

00:05:49.683 --> 00:05:51.226
- For sure.
- With three Tile 7s,

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:54.437
we can get ahead with two points
even if they win a point first.

00:05:54.521 --> 00:05:58.608
So one option is to lock in,
memorize their locations, and secure them.

00:05:58.692 --> 00:05:59.526
Suppose we go second.

00:05:59.609 --> 00:06:01.444
Can we guarantee a Tile 7
with the Move 5 Cells card?

00:06:01.528 --> 00:06:02.362
You got a point.

00:06:02.445 --> 00:06:04.364
- That's right up my alley.
- Great.

00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:05.949
Being on the offensive.

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:07.826
It's very aggressive.
I love that about it.

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:10.078
Let me sum it up for you, Jong-yun.

00:06:10.161 --> 00:06:11.913
Crush their spirits
with your great memory.

00:06:11.997 --> 00:06:13.665
There's no need to overthink it.

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:14.833
All right.

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:17.419
You've got a brilliant memory, anyway.

00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:20.046
Memorizing the board
was central to the game.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.673
There was a movable game piece,

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:26.761
but whoever memorized the 49-tile board
was set to win the match.

00:06:26.845 --> 00:06:30.056
By design, Jong-yun would excel
because of his amazing memory.

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:33.018
It seemed like the perfect chance
for Jong-yun to shine.

00:06:33.101 --> 00:06:35.520
I've trained myself
with the mind palace technique.

00:06:35.604 --> 00:06:38.148
By using this method, I was able
to memorize the 40 double-digit numbers

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:40.358
in under five minutes on the pre-test.

00:06:40.442 --> 00:06:45.780
I think I'm the best at memorization
out of the remaining eight contestants.

00:06:45.864 --> 00:06:49.075
My strategy is to memorize the whole board
so I don't need to check any tiles.

00:06:49.159 --> 00:06:51.703
I'll use all of my 30 seconds
to make my opponent's memory fade.

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:55.373
That's the best game plan
if that doesn't affect your memory.

00:06:55.457 --> 00:06:57.042
What technique do you use again?

00:06:57.125 --> 00:06:58.877
A photographic memory
or creating a sequence?

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.046
Basically, I turn the information
into a sequence.

00:07:01.129 --> 00:07:02.714
How? By making up a story?

00:07:02.797 --> 00:07:03.757
Yes.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.760
I have five compartments in my mind.

00:07:06.843 --> 00:07:08.929
If I want to know the number here,

00:07:09.012 --> 00:07:12.015
I would retrieve it from my memory
by visiting the mental space it is in.

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:13.433
To speed up the process,

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:14.893
I would link the physical location

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:17.979
with my mental compartment
housing that information.

00:07:18.063 --> 00:07:20.649
I'll need to use all 30 seconds anyway,

00:07:20.732 --> 00:07:23.818
and during that time,
my opponent would forget bits and pieces.

00:07:24.152 --> 00:07:25.695
Any tips on memorizing efficiently?

00:07:25.779 --> 00:07:29.324
- That's the key.
- It sure is.

00:07:30.158 --> 00:07:32.869
Memorization...

00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:38.959
Let's say we manage to get a Tile 7
by taking advantage of going second.

00:07:39.042 --> 00:07:41.378
Find pairs whose sum reaches 14 or 21
during the memorization time

00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:44.089
and hold on to their coordinates,
not the individual numbers.

00:07:44.172 --> 00:07:46.758
So our top priority
is the locations of Tile 7s,

00:07:46.841 --> 00:07:48.843
followed by the locations of pairs
that add up to 14?

00:07:48.927 --> 00:07:50.762
Just their locations
without the exact numbers.

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:53.056
We can use these pairs
unless they take one of the tiles.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:56.977
Initially, I thought memorization
would be the most important aspect.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.604
But as you know, there is a limit
to how much you can remember.

00:07:59.688 --> 00:08:03.233
So I needed a foolproof plan to win,
even if my memory wasn't the best.

00:08:03.692 --> 00:08:06.111
Memorizing by pairs seems effective.

00:08:06.194 --> 00:08:07.487
When you have a pair's location

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.782
but end up having to check
because you feel unsure,

00:08:10.865 --> 00:08:13.618
you learn about 2 tiles
while your opponent only learns of 1.

00:08:13.702 --> 00:08:14.786
Oh, that's true.

00:08:14.869 --> 00:08:18.790
If memorization isn't their forte,
their focus will be on the special tiles.

00:08:18.873 --> 00:08:22.293
We should obtain them first
to disrupt their game plan

00:08:22.377 --> 00:08:24.629
and hopefully score two points on our end.

00:08:24.713 --> 00:08:26.089
Here's an idea.

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:27.757
Don't make it your goal

00:08:27.841 --> 00:08:29.342
- to get two-point sets.
- That's the key.

00:08:29.426 --> 00:08:31.136
But you should still
try to get special tiles.

00:08:31.219 --> 00:08:33.471
You can always complete your set
with a suitable basic tile.

00:08:33.555 --> 00:08:36.266
We can then prevent Team KAIST
from scoring two points

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:39.269
- and win 4 times with 1 pointers.
- With steady progress.

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:41.896
If the round drags on,
they'll have to check the tiles.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:44.733
I can go over what I've memorized
as they spend their 30 seconds.

00:08:44.816 --> 00:08:47.277
- I must prolong the game to win.
- Yeah.

00:08:47.694 --> 00:08:49.487
Any volunteers?

00:08:49.571 --> 00:08:51.698
My gut tells me this is your thing.

00:08:52.073 --> 00:08:53.742
- I guess I can do it.
- Good.

00:08:53.825 --> 00:08:55.326
Anyone else?

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.245
I don't think
memorization will be so difficult.

00:08:57.328 --> 00:08:59.873
You mean when you memorize by pairs,
as we discussed?

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:00.790
No, the whole board.

00:09:00.874 --> 00:09:02.292
- Really?
- Yeah.

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:04.294
I can't say how long I'll remember,

00:09:04.377 --> 00:09:05.962
but I think I can manage that
at the start.

00:09:06.713 --> 00:09:08.757
- I'll win Round 1 for us.
- Yes, do that.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.592
Want to take on Round 2, Ji-hoo?

00:09:10.675 --> 00:09:14.804
Team SNU in STEM will send in
Mr. Chess Player and Mr. Hunter.

00:09:14.888 --> 00:09:15.805
Well, probably.

00:09:15.889 --> 00:09:18.516
I can see Mr. Hunter on their roster.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.560
I mean, Dong-geon.

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:23.146
They did great in the pre-test.

00:09:23.229 --> 00:09:25.190
They took most of the top four spots.

00:09:25.273 --> 00:09:26.941
- In STEM Division.
- Yes, exactly.

00:09:27.025 --> 00:09:28.568
Hey, hang on.

00:09:28.651 --> 00:09:31.154
- That doesn't matter.
- Of course not.

00:09:31.946 --> 00:09:34.491
You bet. I can take them.

00:09:34.949 --> 00:09:37.952
I think we'll win.
I've got a good feeling about this.

00:09:38.036 --> 00:09:41.039
- Wait, who's playing in Round 2?
- You guys can sort that out.

00:09:41.122 --> 00:09:43.416
I'll take on Round 3
if Round 1 doesn't turn into a disaster.

00:09:43.833 --> 00:09:45.668
- I can give it a go.
- Go for it if you'd like.

00:09:45.752 --> 00:09:49.172
Wait, will you be using the same strategy?

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:51.716
- I'll decide after--
- He's going to memorize everything.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:55.929
I'll memorize the tiles on the border.
They won't want the piece over there.

00:09:56.012 --> 00:09:59.432
I thought it was very unlikely
they'd memorize the border tiles.

00:09:59.516 --> 00:10:04.854
Since we could move up to three cells,
I could reach the border from any point.

00:10:04.938 --> 00:10:06.022
I could keep up to four tiles.

00:10:06.106 --> 00:10:09.025
Even if I had to pick up tiles
from the section I hadn't memorized,

00:10:09.109 --> 00:10:11.736
I could find a suitable tile
from tapping into my memory

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.030
and complete a set that adds up to 21.

00:10:14.114 --> 00:10:17.283
I thought this would give me
more freedom to maneuver the piece.

00:10:17.367 --> 00:10:19.202
Hey, that's pretty good.

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:21.121
I'll memorize these parts only.

00:10:21.204 --> 00:10:23.414
Can you get to the border from any point?

00:10:23.498 --> 00:10:27.043
Of course. The nearest border cell
will always be within three cells.

00:10:27.127 --> 00:10:27.961
That's true.

00:10:28.044 --> 00:10:30.088
- What if you get crushed?
- Come on.

00:10:30.171 --> 00:10:31.965
I'm going to blindside them.

00:10:32.048 --> 00:10:34.050
A surprise tactic does sound promising.

00:10:34.134 --> 00:10:37.846
Everyone else will focus on the center
because the piece will keep crossing it.

00:10:37.929 --> 00:10:41.099
If I'm going second, I'll be okay
with the border and the special tiles.

00:10:41.182 --> 00:10:43.726
Let's mull it over as we watch Round 1.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:46.354
The time for strategy meeting is up.

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:49.065
You're going to be unbeatable.
Go out there with confidence.

00:10:49.149 --> 00:10:51.067
- I can take them.
- Just be yourself.

00:10:51.151 --> 00:10:54.112
You're not stressed, right?
You've got this. This is your forte.

00:10:54.195 --> 00:10:55.697
I need to deliver here.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:56.823
I know I've got to.

00:10:57.115 --> 00:10:58.741
- SNU!
- For independence!

00:10:59.075 --> 00:11:00.660
- Let's go!
- Let's go!

00:11:02.871 --> 00:11:04.497
DEATHMATCH
LUCKY 21

00:11:04.581 --> 00:11:08.877
We will now begin
the Deathmatch, Lucky 21.

00:11:09.252 --> 00:11:12.338
Players competing in Round 1
are Team KAIST's Jeon Ji-min

00:11:12.422 --> 00:11:15.717
and Team SNU in STEM's Han Jong-yun.

00:11:16.092 --> 00:11:17.886
Please come to the stage.

00:11:21.890 --> 00:11:23.808
Let's have a good game.

00:11:27.187 --> 00:11:30.148
As Team KAIST
held a higher rank in the main match,

00:11:30.231 --> 00:11:32.233
their player will decide who goes first.

00:11:32.483 --> 00:11:36.279
Please make your choice.

00:11:36.738 --> 00:11:39.157
I'll go second.

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:42.243
Team KAIST chose to go second.

00:11:43.036 --> 00:11:45.496
Here is your board for Lucky 21.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.999
You'll be given four minutes to memorize.

00:12:08.019 --> 00:12:09.646
It's best to get Clover Tile 11.

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:11.773
That will play a critical role.

00:12:12.106 --> 00:12:14.192
You can pair it with 8-2, 6-4, or 9-1.

00:12:14.275 --> 00:12:17.028
WITH CLOVER TILE 11,
YOU CAN MAKE 3 SETS THAT ADD UP TO 21

00:12:22.283 --> 00:12:24.244
The time for memorization is over.

00:12:25.495 --> 00:12:28.456
Team SNU in STEM's Han Jong-yun
will go first.

00:12:28.539 --> 00:12:31.501
You can only move one cell
in your initial turn.

00:12:31.584 --> 00:12:34.212
Please move the piece.

00:12:37.882 --> 00:12:41.177
AS PLANNED, HAN JONG-YUN TAKES HIS TIME

00:12:41.594 --> 00:12:43.888
You have ten seconds left.

00:12:47.016 --> 00:12:48.142
I'll obtain the tile.

00:12:51.729 --> 00:12:54.190
HAN JONG-YUN:
CLOVER 8

00:12:54.607 --> 00:12:57.443
He claimed Clover Tile 8.
He can make a 2-pointer set with 9 and 4.

00:12:57.777 --> 00:13:01.406
Team KAIST's Jeon Ji-min.
Please move the piece.

00:13:05.201 --> 00:13:06.786
I'll obtain the tile.

00:13:10.623 --> 00:13:13.543
Smart move.
That Clover Tile 11 will be pivotal.

00:13:13.626 --> 00:13:16.296
BOTH PLAYERS SECURED ONE CLOVER TILE EACH

00:13:16.713 --> 00:13:20.300
